  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Exact item recurrence may be absent after preprocessing and does not capture repeated artists, themes, or tasks across distinct items. Coverage reporting alone does not provide an actionable fallback.",
    "Masking can create unfamiliar internal padding or fully masked attention rows. The report does not specify positional encoding, empty-history handling, truncation, or whether controls exclude targeted positions and preserve the last item.",
    "The audit lacks fixed sample sizes, control repetitions, ranking-change formulas, tie handling, uncertainty estimates, and a GPU-time cap. Its potential score of 8 is optimistic without evidence that diagnostic findings guide validation improvements; implementation difficulty 3 may underestimate these requirements."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether exact recurrence is a useful primary probe and identify an observable fallback when histories contain unique items.",
      "query": "In Amazon sequential recommendation datasets using SASRec preprocessing, how does user-item deduplication affect within-user repeated item coverage? What history-intervention methods identify repeated interests across distinct items using only titles, descriptions, and timestamps, without pretrained weights, and how are those proxies validated?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Establish valid intervention semantics and separate history dependence from padding, position, and last-item artifacts.",
      "query": "For causal SASRec with absolute positional embeddings and left-padded sequences, what artifacts arise from internal zero masking, deletion and repacking, and retention-only history interventions? How should attention masks, final nonpadding readout, empty histories, last-item preservation, and disjoint recency-matched random controls be handled?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Make the audit reproducible and assess whether it provides useful model-selection guidance within the search budget.",
      "query": "How are sequential recommender history-intervention diagnostics evaluated for repeatability and predictive value for held-out ranking improvements? Seek protocols specifying fixed candidate eligibility, top-k overlap and rank-displacement definitions, repeated matched controls, paired uncertainty estimates, and compute-bounded sampling, while keeping sampled HR@10 as the unchanged selection objective."
    }
  ]
}
